==Trivia==
*Forest Playground's Japanese name, Yūen Shinrin Gangūru (遊園森林ガングール, "Amusement Forest Ganguul"), may be a pun on 玩具 ''gangu'' (toy) and ジャングル ''janguru'' (jungle).
*With the exception of [[Beloved Waterfall]], every stage in Forest Playground is a reference to a toy:
**[[Bamboocopter Launch]] refers to the [[wikipedia:Bamboo-copter|bamboo-copter]], a toy helicopter rotor that flies up into the air when its shaft is rapidly spun and released. Another name for this toy is "bamboo dragonfly", which is part of the stage's Japanese name, Taketonbo-gun Kiji (竹とんぼ群生地, "Bamboo Dragonfly Colony").
**[[Broadleaf Yo-Yo]] refers to [[wikipedia:Yo-yo|yo-yos]], which can be made out of wood such as that of a [[wikipedia:Broadleaf|broadleaf tree]].
**[[Marble Swamp]] refers to [[wikipedia:Marble (toy)|marbles]], while its Japanese name, Sokonashi no Ohajiki-numa (底なしのおはじき沼, "Bottomless Ohajiki Swamp") refers to ''[[wikipedia:Ohajiki|ohajiki]]'', a marbles-like children's game in which each player flicks small coin-shaped pieces also called ''ohajiki'' at their opponent's pieces.
**[[Waterweed Trumpet]]'s Japanese name, Piropiro Fue Mizukusa (ピロピロ笛水草, "Party Horn Waterweed"), refers to [[wikipedia:Party horn|party horns]].
**[[Trompo Foothills]] refers to the [[wikipedia:Trompo|trompo]], a top spun by winding a length of string around the body, and launching it so that lands spinning on its point. Its Japanese name, Butsukegoma Kyūryō (ぶつけゴマ丘陵, "Hitting Top Hills"), is a pun on ぶつける ''butsukeru'' (bumping; hitting) and ベイゴマ ''[[wikipedia:Beigoma|beigoma]]'' (a type of spinning top that makes a humming sound when spun).

